"SECTION 1","STRAVA GROUP — ABOVE-STANDARD CARPENTRY SPECIFICATION. Built Once. Built Right."
"SECTION 1","Strava Group is a specialist carpentry division delivering above-standard structural carpentry for builders and clients who prioritise long-term performance, accuracy, and structural integrity over minimum compliance."
"SECTION 1","Strava Group is led by Starvrow (Stav), a licensed carpenter and licensed builder, who began his carpentry apprenticeship at the age of 15. From an early age, the focus was not simply on building houses, but on understanding how buildings behave over time — how they move, where they fail, and why certain construction methods do not last."
"SECTION 1","Over more than a decade in the construction industry, experience has been gained across: High-end residential construction, Complex renovations and structural alterations, Commercial construction, Structural reframing and rectification, Shop fit-outs."
"SECTION 1","Across both new builds and existing homes, the same failures are repeatedly observed: Walls not square or straight from early stages, Frames that move despite meeting minimum standards, Ineffective or failing bracing systems, Twisted studs and plates, Sagging headers and undersized beams, Subfloors that squeak, deflect, or crack finishes above, Waterproofing failures caused by structural movement."
"SECTION 1","In most cases, these failures are not the result of poor materials, but of construction methods that meet minimum standards without accounting for cumulative tolerances, timber behaviour, and long-term load transfer."
"SECTION 1","This document exists to clearly outline how Strava Group deliberately builds above minimum requirements, based on observed failures and long-term performance outcomes."

"SECTION 2","Set-out accuracy is the foundation of the entire structure. If the base is wrong, every stage above compounds that error."
"SECTION 2","Strava Group does not use the traditional 3-4-5 method. While suitable for small layouts, it introduces cumulative error across long wall runs and complex footprints."
"SECTION 2","All structures are squared using true diagonal verification, applying Pythagoras’ theorem and measuring exact corner-to-corner distances."
"SECTION 2","If a building is not square at this stage: Roof geometry will not resolve correctly, Valleys and hips will not sit true, Bird’s mouths and ridge connections will gap, Tiles and flooring will not align with walls, Joinery will not sit true, resulting in uneven gaps and inconsistent reveals."
"SECTION 2","Once set-out is complete, the slab is walked at all wall locations to identify the highest point of the concrete, which becomes the datum for the entire structure."
"SECTION 2","All packing is installed directly on the slab, not at top-plate level."
"SECTION 2","Packing is installed using 90 mm packers and polyurethane adhesive to temporarily secure packers during installation."
"SECTION 2","Packing over 10 mm is permitted where required, however it is never left untreated. Where packing exceeds 10 mm, the area is grouted after installation to ensure full bearing and proper load transfer."
"SECTION 2","Where variations are small and localised, the concrete is ground down or the plate seating area is shaved."
"SECTION 2","Packing at slab level ensures continuous bearing, consistent sill heights, accurate reference points, and proper load transfer. Packing at top plate level only disguises issues."

"SECTION 3","All studs are individually checked prior to installation. Stud bows are identified, oriented in the same direction, and clearly marked."
"SECTION 3","Only straight plates are used. Bowed or twisted plates are rejected. All plates are cut on a drop saw to eliminate cumulative error."
"SECTION 3","Bent plates lead to wavy walls and joinery not sitting true. It is significantly harder to correct a bowed plate later."
"SECTION 3","Wall frames are assembled using rear nailing, side fixing, and structural screws to all end studs. Noggings are fixed the same way."
"SECTION 3","Typical frames can disconnect with a hammer strike. Strava Group frames remain locked even before structural screws are fully installed."
"SECTION 3","All junctions are reinforced. Studs are pre-locked and additional fixings are added when walls are joined to prevent movement."

"SECTION 4","Windows and doors are common failure points due to poor load transfer."
"SECTION 4","Single headers and nail-only fixing allow sag and movement over time."
"SECTION 4","Strava Group uses double headers on all openings regardless of minimum engineering."
"SECTION 4","Headers are glued with polyurethane adhesive, screwed together, and fixed into studs using both screws and nails."
"SECTION 4","Spans over 3m receive double jack studs and doubled sill plates."

"SECTION 5","Bracing is treated as a primary structural system, not a compliance item."
"SECTION 5","Traditional angled bracing cuts into studs, weakens them, and relies on compression."
"SECTION 5","Angled bracing is faster and cheaper but allows movement."
"SECTION 5","Strava Group uses tension-based bracing systems."
"SECTION 5","Tension bracing requires temporary bracing, more time, and final tensioning, but provides superior performance."
"SECTION 5","It engages all four corners, restrains movement, and remains effective over time."
"SECTION 5","Angled bracing allows walls to be shaken with minimal force. Tension bracing eliminates this movement."
"SECTION 5","Hoop iron bracing is installed using engineered clouts."
"SECTION 5","Bracing ends are tied using straps or Pryda screws."
"SECTION 5","Concrete bracing points use 150 mm N16 epoxy threaded rods."
"SECTION 5","Timber subfloors use structural screws and bottom plates are screwed and nailed."
"SECTION 5","Plywood bracing uses correct nailing patterns, structural screws, and M16 epoxy rods at corners."

"SECTION 6","Subfloor issues originate before sheets are installed."
"SECTION 6","Level base prevents squeaks and deflection."
"SECTION 6","Joists are checked with a straightedge and planed flat before sheeting."
"SECTION 6","Double beams are laminated with polyurethane and bolted."
"SECTION 6","Double i-joists under load-bearing walls are avoided."
"SECTION 6","Liquid nails is not used. Polyurethane adhesive with a 30 mm bead is used instead."
"SECTION 6","Sheets are fixed with ring-shank nails and screw-fixed after installation."
"SECTION 6","All joints are glued and expansion joints installed."
"SECTION 6","Steel-to-timber connections use packing, adhesive, bolts, brackets, and structural screws."
"SECTION 6","Adhesive is applied in brackets to prevent squeaks."
"SECTION 6","Subfloor includes tensioned X-bracing to tie the structure together."

"SECTION 7","Roof accuracy depends entirely on framing below."
"SECTION 7","Unlevel plates result in wavy roofs. Correct base produces straight roofs."
"SECTION 7","All walls receive double top plates, including single-storey homes."
"SECTION 7","Birdsmouth cuts are not overcut and are hand finished."
"SECTION 7","Overcutting weakens timber, especially in 90 mm framing."
"SECTION 7","Rafters are fixed with nails and Pryda rafter screws to prevent slippage."
"SECTION 7","Struts and purlins are accurately cut and fixed with nails and screws."
"SECTION 7","Roof includes pre-camber up to 10 mm to prevent sag under load."
"SECTION 7","Collar ties are installed where possible."

"SECTION 8","All works comply with engineering, NCC, and Australian Standards."
"SECTION 8","Enhanced measures do not replace engineering design."